# P3 bidirectionality verification log (#135 — the schematic split)

Worktree: .worktrees/phase-p3, branch feature/phase-p3.
Baseline HEAD at verification: c0db95b (all P3 work committed before any mutation).
Discipline: every mutant COMPILES clean (tsc -b exit 0), fails via a GENUINE
assertion naming the new/re-pinned test, then git checkout restores green. A
green mutant would be a finding to investigate — none occurred.

Baseline: npm test → 46 files, 1227 tests PASS. npm run check → clean.

---

## B1 — the :735 output-arrow anchor parameterization (the r1 HIGH)

New behaviour: the output break-out arrows' top endpoint anchors at
`machineTop + rulerH`, NOT the old `+ 40` literal (which the risen outputTop
now equals — the coincidence trap that would float the arrows in the lane).

- PASS (clean): smoke "anchors output belt-arrows at machineTop + rulerH …
  (P3 register pin)" green.
- MUTANT (compiles): Schematic.tsx `machineTopY={machineTopY + LAYOUT.rulerH}`
  → `machineTopY={machineTopY + 40}`. tsc -b exit 0.
- FAIL (genuine): register pin → `AssertionError: expected 140 to be 112`
  (140 = machineTop 100 + 40 = the output lane top; 112 = machineTop 100 +
  rulerH 12 = the row bottom). Falsifiable against the coincidence, exactly as
  the r2 adversarial proved.
- RESTORE: git checkout src/ui/Schematic.tsx → pin green.

## B2 — the un-gated significant (the r2 registration fix)

New behaviour: `significant` computes in BOTH density modes, not just band.

- PASS (clean): layout "computes the significant set BELOW the threshold too
